-- BeeAware Brasil roadmap / Phase 4 — RJ Geo, RISP boundaries.
--
-- Real ISP-RJ territorial boundaries (roadmap 4.3's RjGeoAdapter), for
-- the "user GPS -> ST_Contains() -> CISP -> AISP -> RISP -> matching
-- statistics" spatial join the roadmap describes — RjIspAdapter's own
-- CSV (BaseDPEvolucaoMensalCisp.csv) already has cisp/aisp/risp columns
-- per row, but with no polygon geometry to join against, so today it can
-- only aggregate straight up to MUNICIPALITY (see rj_isp.ts's own header:
-- "no CISP-level geometry loaded to make finer granularity meaningful
-- yet"). This is that geometry.
--
-- Source: https://www.ispdados.rj.gov.br/Conteudo.html, the KML exports
-- under "Divisão Territorial da Base de Segurança" — RISPkml.rar,
-- AISPkml.rar, CISPkml.rar (2026 boundaries for AISP/CISP, 2019 for
-- RISP, per the page's own labels). Verified live 2026-08-25: all three
-- downloaded (200 OK, genuine RAR5 archives, Last-Modified 2026-08-14),
-- extracted, and parsed as real KML — 7 RISP / 41 AISP / 137 CISP
-- placemarks, each carrying its own id and (for AISP/CISP) its parent
-- id as ExtendedData/SimpleData fields, confirmed as a clean hierarchy
-- with no orphans (41 AISPs span exactly 7 distinct RISP values, 137
-- CISPs span exactly 41 distinct AISP values).
--
-- This is a one-off manual population, same reasoning and same caveat
-- as 20260825150000_sinesp_manual_population.sql: RAR is not a format
-- Deno's Edge Function runtime can decompress (no built-in support, no
-- established pure-JS/Deno RAR5 decoder), so there is currently no
-- registered RjGeoAdapter that could re-fetch this automatically. ISP's
-- own page labels these as fixed-year boundary sets (2019/2026) rather
-- than a frequently-revised dataset, so this is expected to stay valid
-- far longer than SINESP's monthly-refreshed data did — but it is still
-- a manual snapshot, not a recurring job, and would need a genuine
-- RAR-capable fetch path (or a re-run of this same external
-- download-and-convert step) to pick up ISP's next boundary revision.
--
-- Coordinates were rounded from the source's 13-decimal-place precision
-- (sub-millimeter, meaningless for administrative boundaries) down to 6
-- decimals (~11cm) purely to keep the migration size reasonable — not a
-- shape simplification, just trimming digits with no real-world
-- significance. ST_MakeValid repairs a handful of degenerate self-touch
-- points present in ISP's own source geometry (2 of 7 RISPs, 2 of 41
-- AISPs, 2 of 137 CISPs — same coordinate pairs recur at every level,
-- confirmed area change is ~0 via Shapely's own make_valid before this
-- was trusted) — without it, ST_Contains could give wrong answers right
-- at those points.
--
-- RISP is inserted first (no parent) since AISP/CISP's parent_area_id
-- lookups (in the migrations that follow this one) join against RISP
-- rows already being in the table by name ('RISP {id}').

-- Generated from real RISP KML data (ispdados.rj.gov.br) — 7 rows.
insert into geo_areas (country_code, state_code, area_type, name, geometry, parent_area_id, source, source_version)
select v.country_code, v.state_code, v.area_type::geo_area_type, v.name,
  ST_MakeValid(ST_SetSRID(ST_GeomFromText(v.wkt), 4326)),
  null,
  v.source, v.source_version
from (values
